---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';
import Detail from '../../components/Detail.astro';

// PATTERN REFERENCE for the /fix articles.
//
// The old version answered the easy 80% and stopped exactly where the reader
// gets stuck: Windows not offering FAT32 above 32GB, and macOS hiding the
// scheme selector unless you pick the device rather than the volume. Both send
// people away, and both are the actual reason a "successfully formatted" drive
// is refused at the booth.
//
// The fix is completeness WITHOUT a wall of text. The answer and the 60-second
// path stay visible; every branch after that is collapsed, so a DJ reads only
// the one that applies to them. Collapsed content is still in the HTML, so
// Google indexes it and AI answer engines quote it exactly as if it were open.
import { CHECKED } from '../../data/facts.js';
const UPDATED = CHECKED;

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'HowTo',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/format-usb-for-cdj#howto',
  name: 'How to format a USB for Pioneer CDJ (FAT32 + MBR)',
  description: 'Format a USB drive so every Pioneer CDJ and XDJ reads it: FAT32 file system, MBR partition scheme, on both macOS and Windows, including drives over 32GB.',
  totalTime: 'PT10M',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-20',
  dateModified: '2026-07-26',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/format-usb-for-cdj#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/format-usb-for-cdj.png',
  supply: [{ '@type': 'HowToSupply', name: 'A 32-64 GB USB drive' }],
  tool: [{ '@type': 'HowToTool', name: 'macOS Disk Utility or Windows Format' }],
  // THE STEPS ARE NOW THE PAGE'S OWN WORDS. Rewritten 2026-08-04.
  //
  // They used to be paraphrases: "On macOS use Disk Utility with Show All
  // Devices enabled and select the device, not the volume" appears nowhere on
  // this page. That is the same defect that got the FAQPage blocks deleted
  // from the homepage and /emergency, and Google applies the same rule to
  // HowTo: the steps must be the steps the reader can see. Each `text` below
  // is the matching item in "The 60-second version", plus the warning panel
  // above it, word for word, minus markup.
  //
  // Keep them in step. If you edit the visible <ol class="art-steps">, edit
  // this array in the same commit.
  step: [
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Copy everything off it first',
      text: 'Formatting erases the drive permanently. Copy everything off it first.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Mac',
      text: 'Mac: Disk Utility → View → Show All Devices → select the drive, not the volume → Erase → MS-DOS (FAT) + Master Boot Record.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Windows',
      text: 'Windows: right-click the drive in Explorer → Format → FAT32 → quick format is fine on a drive you trust. On a drive you suspect, untick quick and let the full format run: it works the whole drive, so it doubles as a stress test.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Put music on it with rekordbox',
      text: 'Put music on it with rekordbox 7.2.13 or later in EXPORT mode. Do not drag files.',
    },
    {
      '@type': 'HowToStep',
      name: 'Test it on a player',
      text: 'Load a track from it on a player before the drive matters.',
    },
  ],
};

// The FAQPage block that used to sit here was removed on 2026-08-04: its
// questions and answers were paraphrases that did not appear verbatim in the
// visible page text, which is exactly what Google's structured data policy
// forbids. The HowTo schema above had the SAME defect and was rewritten the
// same day rather than deleted: the procedure genuinely is on the page and is
// the most citable thing on it, it was only the WORDING that was invented.
// It now quotes the page.
---
<Base
  title="Format a USB for Pioneer CDJ: FAT32 + MBR | Save My Gig"
  description="FAT32 file system, MBR partition scheme: the settings every Pioneer CDJ and XDJ reads. Plus the two traps, Windows hiding FAT32 and Mac hiding Scheme."
  path="/fix/format-usb-for-cdj"
  crumbName="Format a USB"
  ogImage="/og/format-usb-for-cdj.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />

  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <nav class="crumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/">Home</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <a href="/faq">Fixes</a> <span aria-hidden="true">/</span> <span>Format a USB</span>
    </nav>

    <div class="status-line">Format_Guide</div>
    <h1 class="art-h1">Format a USB for <span class="accent">CDJ</span></h1>
    <p class="dim art-updated">Last verified: {UPDATED} · about 10 minutes</p>

    <div class="panel panel-go">
      <p style="margin:0">
        <strong>The answer:</strong> <span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32</span> file system,
        <span class="mono" translate="no">MBR</span> partition scheme, on a 32&ndash;64&nbsp;GB drive.
        Every rekordbox-era CDJ and XDJ reads FAT32, and MBR is the scheme we
        recommend (the why is below).
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="panel panel-warn" style="margin-top:0.8rem">
      <p style="margin:0">Formatting <strong>erases the drive permanently</strong>. Copy everything off it first.</p>
    </div>

    <h2 class="art-h2">The 60-second version</h2>
    <ol class="art-steps">
      <li><strong>Mac:</strong> Disk Utility &rarr; View &rarr; <strong>Show All Devices</strong> &rarr; select the <strong>drive</strong>, not the volume &rarr; Erase &rarr; <span class="mono" translate="no">MS-DOS (FAT)</span> + <span class="mono" translate="no">Master Boot Record</span>.</li>
      <li><strong>Windows:</strong> right-click the drive in Explorer &rarr; Format &rarr; <span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32</span> &rarr; quick format is fine on a drive you trust. On a drive you suspect, untick quick and let the full format run: it works the whole drive, so it doubles as a stress test.</li>
      <li>Put music on it with <strong>rekordbox 7.2.13 or later in EXPORT mode</strong>. Do not drag files.</li>
      <li>Load a track from it on a player <strong>before the drive matters</strong>.</li>
    </ol>

    <h2 class="art-h2">If something goes wrong</h2>
    <p class="dim art-lead">Open only the one that describes you.</p>

    <Detail
      q="FAT32 isn't in the list on Windows"
      hint="Almost always a drive bigger than 32GB. Three ways out."
    >
      <p>
        This is a limit in the <strong>Windows formatting dialog</strong>, not in FAT32.
        FAT32 itself supports volumes up to <strong>2&nbsp;TB</strong>. Microsoft simply
        stopped offering it above 32&nbsp;GB in the graphical tool. Pick whichever of
        these suits you:
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Use a free formatter.</strong> <span class="mono" translate="no">guiformat</span> (or its command-line twin <span class="mono" translate="no">fat32format</span>), both from Ridgecrop Consultants, will format a large drive to FAT32 in a few seconds. This is what most DJs end up using.</li>
        <li><strong>Windows 11 command line.</strong> Microsoft lifted the built-in <span class="mono" translate="no">format</span> command's FAT32 limit to 2&nbsp;TB (announced for Windows 11 in 2024), while the graphical dialog still caps at 32&nbsp;GB. Open a terminal as administrator and run <span class="mono" translate="no">format X: /FS:FAT32 /Q</span>, replacing X with your drive letter. Check the letter twice; if your build refuses, guiformat above does the same job.</li>
        <li><strong>Shrink it.</strong> Disk Management &rarr; shrink the volume to 32&nbsp;GB &rarr; format that partition FAT32. You lose the rest of the capacity, which for a gig drive rarely matters.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The tempting alternative is exFAT, because Windows offers it right there in the
        dropdown. Do not take it unless you know every player you will touch reads exFAT.
        <a href="/fix/exfat-vs-fat32-cdj">Here is which players do &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </Detail>

    <Detail
      q="There's no Scheme option in Mac Disk Utility"
      hint="You selected the volume instead of the drive."
    >
      <p>
        Disk Utility hides the partition scheme unless you are looking at the
        <strong>physical device</strong>. By default it only shows you the volume
        sitting on top of it.
      </p>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>In Disk Utility, choose <strong>View &rarr; Show All Devices</strong>.</li>
        <li>The sidebar now shows the drive with the volume <strong>indented underneath it</strong>.</li>
        <li>Select the <strong>top-level entry</strong>, the one with the manufacturer name, not the indented one.</li>
        <li>Click Erase. The <strong>Scheme</strong> dropdown now appears. Choose <span class="mono" translate="no">Master Boot Record</span>.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This matters more than it sounds. Erasing only the volume leaves the existing
        partition map in place, which on any modern drive is <strong>GPT</strong>. The
        format reports success, the drive mounts perfectly on your Mac, and the CDJ
        refuses it. That is the single most common "but it formatted fine" story.
      </p>
    </Detail>

    <Detail
      q="It formatted fine but the CDJ still won't read it"
      hint="Four causes, in the order worth checking."
    >
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>The scheme is GPT, not MBR.</strong> The usual suspect, and invisible from the computer. Re-erase selecting the device rather than the volume (see above). On Windows check Disk Management &rarr; right-click the disk &rarr; Properties &rarr; Volumes. AlphaTheta documents this for specific models: their support articles state that the CDJ-3000 (article 4406135001625) and the XDJ-AN (article 53214453591009) do not support the GUID Partition Map. For the rest of the range no scheme is published, so MBR is the manufacturer's documented answer where they name one and our recommendation everywhere else. It costs nothing and it removes a variable.</li>
        <li><strong>It is exFAT, not FAT32.</strong> Drives above 32&nbsp;GB ship exFAT from the factory, so a brand-new "professional" 64&nbsp;GB stick fails on an older CDJ out of the box. If you are using an <strong>SD card</strong> rather than a USB stick, exFAT is not an option at all: rekordbox will not export to an exFAT SD card.</li>
        <li><strong>The music was dragged on, not exported.</strong> The player may show nothing at all in playlist view. Try the <strong>FOLDER</strong> browse button: if the tracks are there, that is what happened.</li>
        <li><strong>The drive itself is failing.</strong> If it formats but errors partway through copying, stop trusting it. <a href="/fix/dj-usb-backup-strategy">Two identical drives, always &rarr;</a></li>
      </ul>
    </Detail>

    <Detail
      q="How do I know it actually worked?"
      hint="Thirty seconds now beats finding out in the booth."
    >
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li><strong>Check the format.</strong> Mac: select the drive in Disk Utility, it should read <span class="mono" translate="no">MS-DOS (FAT32)</span> and <span class="mono" translate="no">Master Boot Record</span>. Windows: right-click in Explorer &rarr; Properties &rarr; File system should say <span class="mono" translate="no">FAT32</span>.</li>
        <li><strong>Export, then eject properly.</strong> Use rekordbox's own eject button. Pulling the drive mid-write is the most common way to corrupt the database you just built.</li>
        <li><strong>Load a track on a real player.</strong> Your own, or arrive early enough to test on the club's. A drive that has never been tested is not a backup, it is a hope.</li>
      </ul>
    </Detail>

    <Detail
      q="What size drive should I buy?"
      hint="32-64 GB. Bigger is worse here."
    >
      <p>
        32&ndash;64&nbsp;GB is the sweet spot: big enough for a full working library,
        small enough that FAT32 formats without a fight. Larger drives take longer to
        format, longer for a player to scan, and buy you nothing for a single gig.
      </p>
      <p>
        The FAT32 limit worth knowing is <strong>4&nbsp;GB per file</strong>, not per
        drive. A 320kbps MP3 is around 9&nbsp;MB, and even a long uncompressed WAV
        rarely passes 1&nbsp;GB, so for a music library it never comes up. FAT32 has
        other ceilings, a 2&nbsp;TB volume limit and the per-model file and folder
        counts AlphaTheta publishes in support article 4406512510105, but none of
        them reach a 32&ndash;64&nbsp;GB gig drive either.
        <a href="/fix/best-usb-size-for-djing">More on sizing &rarr;</a> ·
        <a href="/gear">Drives that behave &rarr;</a>
      </p>
    </Detail>

    <h2 class="art-h2">The rule worth remembering</h2>
    <p class="art-close">
      <strong>Format for the oldest player you might ever face, not the most modern one you
      own.</strong> The festival main stage has CDJ-3000s. The afterparty basement has a
      CDJ-2000 with a cigarette burn on it. FAT32 plus MBR plays both.
    </p>

    <ArticleCTA article="format_guide" />
  </main>
</Base>

